    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-a-macdonald"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-a-macdonald.jpg" alt="John A. Macdonald" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-a-macdonald">John A. Macdonald</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_A_Macdonald_(ca._1875).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Lancefield, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> was created by Confederation, the union of British North American colonies agreed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlottetown-conference">Charlottetown</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> conferences of 1864 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Conference of 1866. Its architects, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-a-macdonald">John A. Macdonald</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-etienne-cartier">George-Étienne Cartier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-brown-canadian-politician">George Brown</a>, wanted to escape the political deadlock of the Province of Canada, where English-speaking Canada West and French-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada-east">Canada East</a> had been joined since 1841, to build a railway linking the interior to the Atlantic, and to secure the colonies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, whose army had emerged from the Civil War as one of the largest in the world and from which Fenian raiders crossed the border in 1866. The British Parliament passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-act-1867">British North America Act</a>, and on 1 July 1867 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of Canada came into being, uniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>, New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and the Province of Canada, which was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and Quebec. Macdonald became the first prime minister, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawa">Ottawa</a>, chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> in 1857 as capital of the Province of Canada, became the capital of the new dominion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-edward-island">Prince Edward Island</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> had sent delegates to Quebec but at first stayed out, and the new country of about 3.5 million people occupied only a small part of its future territory.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harold-innis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harold-innis.jpg" alt="Harold Innis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harold-innis">Harold Innis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harold_Innis_public-domain_library_archives-canada.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">H. James that is a pseudonym for William James Jr. according to City of Toroto Archive, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of its history <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>’s economy rested on the export of staples, first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and wheat, then minerals, pulp and paper and, in recent decades, oil and gas, a pattern analysed by the economist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-innis">Harold Innis</a>. The Canadian Pacific Railway, completed in 1885, and Macdonald’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-policy">National Policy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protective-tariff">protective tariffs</a> from 1879 were meant to bind the country together from east to west, with the industries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> supplying settlers in the west. Large-scale immigration under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid-laurier">Wilfrid Laurier</a>’s government after 1896, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a>, Germans, Scandinavians and Americans as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britons-celtic-people">Britons</a>, settled the prairies, which became one of the world’s great wheat-growing regions, while Chinese labourers who had helped build the railway faced a head tax from 1885 and near-total exclusion from 1923. Quebec remained a largely Catholic, rural and French-speaking society until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quiet-revolution">Quiet Revolution</a> of the 1960s secularised and modernised it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nations-in-canada">First Nations</a> on the prairies lost most of their lands through the numbered treaties of 1871–1921, and many Indigenous children were sent to residential schools, which a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-of-canada">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a> described in 2015 as cultural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. After 1945 Canada built a welfare state, including public health insurance in every province by the early 1970s, and its economy became closely integrated with that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, culminating in free trade agreements in 1988 and 1994.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-batoche"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-batoche.jpg" alt="Battle of Batoche" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-batoche">Battle of Batoche</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Batoche_Print_by_Seargent_Grundy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Seargent Grundy, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The young dominion relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for its defence, and its first operations were internal: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-expedition-1870">Wolseley Expedition</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-colony">Red River</a> in 1870 after the resistance led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a>, and the suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-rebellion">North-West Rebellion</a> of 1885, when troops moved west on the unfinished Canadian Pacific Railway defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-batoche">Battle of Batoche</a>; Riel was hanged later that year. Canadian volunteers served in the South African War of 1899–1902. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> some 620,000 Canadians enlisted and about 60,000 died; the Canadian Corps won a celebrated victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vimy-ridge">Battle of Vimy Ridge</a> in April 1917 and spearheaded the Hundred Days offensive of 1918, while the conscription crisis of 1917 split English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-canadians">French Canada</a>. In September 1939 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> separately from Britain and went on to enlist more than a million men and women; its army fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juno-beach">Juno Beach</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normandy-landings">D-Day</a>, its navy fought throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-atlantic">Battle of the Atlantic</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-commonwealth-air-training-plan">British Commonwealth Air Training Plan</a> trained over 130,000 aircrew in Canada. A founding member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1949, Canada fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the North American Air Defence Command in 1958, took part in many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> peacekeeping missions, and fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> from 2001.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-act-1867"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-act-1867.jpg" alt="Constitution Act, 1867" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-act-1867">Constitution Act, 1867</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_North_America_Act,_1867.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Statute enacted by the Parliament of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland; Published by Hunter, Rose in Ottawa, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-act-1867">British North America Act</a> of 1867 created a federation with a parliamentary system on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster model</a>: a Governor General representing the Crown, an elected House of Commons, an appointed Senate, and provinces with their own legislatures and powers over education, property and civil rights, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> kept its French-derived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>. Macdonald, prime minister in 1867–1873 and 1878–1891, expanded the dominion by acquiring Rupert’s Land and the North-Western Territory from the Hudson’s Bay Company in 1870, creating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> joined in 1871 on the promise of a railway, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-edward-island">Prince Edward Island</a> in 1873. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a> became provinces in 1905, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> joined after referendums in 1949. Autonomy grew in stages: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> in its own right and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration-of-1926">Balfour Declaration of 1926</a> recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> as equal in status to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-westminster-1931">Statute of Westminster</a> of 1931 gave them legislative independence. Canadian citizenship was created in 1947, and appeals to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judicial-committee-of-the-privy-council">Judicial Committee of the Privy Council</a> ended in 1949. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-act-1982">Constitution Act, 1982</a>, patriated the constitution with a domestic amending formula and the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms. The monarch of the United Kingdom remains King of Canada, and federal politics has been dominated by the Liberal and Conservative parties.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights.jpg" alt="Universal Declaration of Human Rights" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights">Universal Declaration of Human Rights</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eleanor_Roosevelt_UDHR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDR Presidential Library &amp; Museum, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> was the first self-governing dominion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, and its gradual, negotiated path from colony to independence, through responsible government, Confederation and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-westminster-1931">Statute of Westminster</a>, became a model for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and for the later transformation of the empire into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth of Nations</a>. Canadian diplomats helped build post-war institutions: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lester-b-pearson">Lester B. Pearson</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1957 for proposing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-emergency-force">United Nations Emergency Force</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a>, which set the pattern for armed peacekeeping, and the lawyer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-peters-humphrey">John Peters Humphrey</a> prepared the first draft of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights">Universal Declaration of Human Rights</a>. Canada adopted official bilingualism in 1969 and in 1971 became the first country to make multiculturalism official policy, approaches that influenced debates elsewhere about diversity and national identity. Its public health care system has been widely studied and debated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/insulin">Insulin</a> was discovered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> in 1921–1922 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-banting">Frederick Banting</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-best">Charles Best</a> with their colleagues, and thinkers and writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-mcluhan">Marshall McLuhan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-atwood">Margaret Atwood</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alice-munro">Alice Munro</a>, Nobel laureate in 2013, reached international audiences. As a long-standing member of the G7, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the Commonwealth, and a major exporter of energy, minerals and food, Canada has exercised the influence of a significant middle power.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Inventions and discoveries credited here</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/inventions?focus=insulin">Insulin<span>1921</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="Second World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-646-5188-17,_Flugzeuge_Junkers_Ju_87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Opitz, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> never ended; it became the modern sovereign Canada through gradual constitutional evolution rather than revolution. The term dominion fell out of official use after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, the Maple Leaf flag replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canadian-red-ensign">Red Ensign</a> in 1965, and the patriation of the constitution in 1982 removed the last legal power of the British Parliament over Canada, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>’s government never endorsed the settlement. The most serious challenge to the federation came from Quebec nationalism. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-de-liberation-du-quebec">Front de libération du Québec</a> carried out bombings and, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">October Crisis</a> of 1970, kidnappings that led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-trudeau">Pierre Trudeau</a> to invoke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-measures-act">War Measures Act</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parti-quebecois">Parti Québécois</a> held referendums on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in 1980, defeated by about 60 to 40 per cent, and in 1995, defeated by only 50.6 to 49.4 per cent. The creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nunavut">Nunavut</a> in 1999, modern land claims agreements and the recognition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal</a> and treaty rights in section 35 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-act-1982">Constitution Act, 1982</a>, began to reshape relations with Indigenous peoples. Today Canada is a federation of ten provinces and three territories with about 41 million people, the second-largest country in the world by area, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-prince-of-monaco">Charles III</a>, with an economy deeply integrated with that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> matters as the first self-governing dominion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, a federation that grew from four colonies into the world’s second-largest country and won full independence by peaceful constitutional evolution, while balancing English and French communities and confronting the dispossession of Indigenous peoples.</em></p>

